ust as your two main characters did  i i you know what was important for me  was to have eyewitnesses or to you know  people who could add something to the  story  um i i don't know that the jackson  family  has any direct knowledge of what  happened to wade and james i don't  believe they do if they do then they  should  this was clearly very difficult to watch  with many disturbing details  how do you vet some of these details  though because  most of this happened with two people in  the room according to these two men  and that's the case with a lot of  pedophile allegations is you know it's  two people in a room and often people  only come out many years after the  sexual abuse has taken place  um you know i did a deep dive into the  the  invest the child sexual abuse  allegations against jackson in 93 and in  2003 2005 and read a lot of the  the witness statements there and spoke  to a lot of the investigators and i  didn't find anything that contradicted  or cast any doubt whatsoever on wade and  james's accounts  the documentary was also very graphic  why did you decide to go that way  the details of it i think for many years  jackson got away with  this image of being a bit of a child  himself and you know being very  affectionate with children and  i wanted to make sure that people  understood this wasn't  over enthusiastic you know kissing or  cuddling this was  sex this was the kind of sex adults have  but he was having it with  a little a little child and he was known  to spend a lot of time  with with children some famous macaulay  culkin  corey feldman they have throughout the  years denied  any sexual abuse took place did you walk  into this project  objectively or did you walk in already  believing  these two gentlemen you know i had no  prior views about michael jackson i  i had never taken a special interest in  jackson or anyone famous you know i come  from a background of making films about  terrorism and crime  um and so i came to it with a com